  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposed HMO by reason of its limited overall floor space, limited communal kitchen space layout as well as its limited natural lighting and ventilation impacts within the kitchen that are associated with the proposed increased occupancy at the site, would represent a substandard and inappropriate form of accommodation which would not be capable of meeting the reasonable needs of its occupiers and would be detrimental to the standard of living of the occupants. The scheme therefore fails to provide an adequate standard of residential accommodation and it is contrary to Policy D6 of the London Plan (2021), Core Strategy Policies CP4 and CP30 (2010), Development Management Document Policies DMD5 and DMD37 (2014), as well as the NPPF (2026).",
    "The proposed HMO development would intensify the residential use at the site, thereby resulting in an increase in comings and goings with associated noise impacts to the neighbouring occupiers without providing appropriate noise insulation for mitigation measures.  The scheme therefore fails to safeguard residential amenity and it is contrary to Policy D3 of the London Plan (2021), Core Strategy Policies CP4 and CP30, Development Management Document Policies DMD5, DMD37 and DMD 68 as well as the NPPF (2026).",
    "The proposed development fails to provide sufficient details by way of a parking survey to assess the parking impacts of the development. It has therefore not been demonstrated that the proposal would not result in unacceptable parking stress on the surrounding highway network. The proposals are thereby contrary to Local Plan Policies CP24 and CP30 (2010), Development Management Plan Policy DMD45 (2014), Policies T5 and T6.1 of the London Plan (2021) and the guidance contained in the NPPF (2026).",
    " The proposed first floor rear extension by reason of its excessive depth, design, siting and scale would constitute an obtrusive and overly dominant form of development, resulting in an unacceptable loss of light, outlook, and an increased sense of enclosure detrimental to the occupiers of the adjoining properties. Additionally, the design, bulk and scale of the extension would be out of keeping and character with the surrounding form and pattern of development within the terrace of which the site forms a part. In this respect, the proposal is thereby contrary to Policy CP30 of the Core Strategy (2010), DMD8, DMD11 and DMD37 of the Development Management Document (2014), D3 of the London Plan (2021) and the guidance contained within the National Planning Policy Framework (2026)."
  ],
